1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,262
You install a web server. It works, you go home.

2
00:00:03,282 --> 00:00:07,344
The server reboots at night, and in the morning the website is gone.

3
00:00:07,364 --> 00:00:09,745
One missing command. Today you learn it.

4
00:00:10,215 --> 00:00:11,046
In this video:

5
00:00:11,066 --> 00:00:15,849
start and stop services, and configure services to start automatically at boot.

6
00:00:20,369 --> 00:00:22,952
Here's the task, the way an exam would state it.

7
00:00:23,422 --> 00:00:28,287
Start and stop services and configure services to start automatically at boot.

8
00:00:28,757 --> 00:00:32,300
We'll start and stop a web server, then make it survive a reboot.

9
00:00:38,820 --> 00:00:44,951
On Linux, a service is a program that runs in the background, like a web server.

10
00:00:44,971 --> 00:00:49,488
System D manages it, and the tool you talk to is systemctl.

11
00:00:53,958 --> 00:00:57,999
Two separate questions. Is it running now? That is start and stop.

12
00:00:58,019 --> 00:01:01,384
Will it run after a reboot? That is enable and disable.

13
00:01:01,404 --> 00:01:04,583
They do not affect each other, and that is the trap.

14
00:01:11,053 --> 00:01:16,450
Before the demo, silently, the lab installs the Apache web server package,

15
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:19,524
named httpd. This step changes the system.

16
00:01:28,044 --> 00:01:31,234
First, look. Systemctl status shows the service.

17
00:01:31,254 --> 00:01:36,651
Loaded says where its unit file is, and disabled means it will not start at boot.

18
00:01:36,671 --> 00:01:39,928
Active inactive dead means it is not running now.

19
00:01:49,448 --> 00:01:53,374
Now start it. Sudo, because starting services needs root.

20
00:01:53,394 --> 00:01:59,119
Is dash active answers with one word: active. Restart stops it and starts it again.

21
00:01:59,139 --> 00:02:02,719
Stop stops it, and is dash active now says inactive.

22
00:02:14,239 --> 00:02:17,918
Now the boot question. Is dash enabled says disabled.

23
00:02:17,938 --> 00:02:23,500
Enable creates a link, a symlink, that makes system D start the service at boot.

24
00:02:23,520 --> 00:02:25,803
Is dash enabled now says enabled.

25
00:02:33,273 --> 00:02:37,821
But look: is dash active still says inactive. Enable did not start it.

26
00:02:37,841 --> 00:02:42,128
Remember the two questions: enable changes the next boot, not now.

27
00:02:49,648 --> 00:02:51,981
The fast way: enable dash dash now.

28
00:02:52,001 --> 00:02:56,753
It enables the service and starts it, in one command. Now it is active.

29
00:03:04,273 --> 00:03:07,709
Our five steps: task, demo, check, reboot, and your turn.

30
00:03:09,229 --> 00:03:14,046
We reboot. Enabled, and active. Nobody started the web server this time.

31
00:03:14,066 --> 00:03:15,524
System D did, at boot.

32
00:03:25,044 --> 00:03:29,295
Your turn. Two services. Make httpd run now and at every boot.

33
00:03:29,315 --> 00:03:34,461
And the at daemon, A T D: stop it, and make sure it does not start at boot.

34
00:03:34,481 --> 00:03:35,563
Run the checker.

35
00:03:43,033 --> 00:03:44,783
Try before you open the solution.

36
00:03:50,303 --> 00:03:54,763
Next up: configure systems to boot into a specific target automatically.

37
00:03:55,233 --> 00:03:58,136
Subscribe to follow the series, and let's keep going.
